{"title":"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 24","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Note\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Note</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(AoPS_Precalculus)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (AoPS Precalculus)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(quick_but_risky)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (quick but risky)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Unit-period_parametrization)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Unit-period parametrization)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_5_(Risky)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5 (Risky)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_2_by_OmegaLearn.org\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 2 by OmegaLearn.org</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_3_by_MadMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 3 by MadMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>How many real numbers satisfy the equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/0/c306f0b9328d962e79d0d443c1481c26ffefda96.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin(20\\pi x) = \\log_{20}(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"164\" height=\"20\" />?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/2/de2dd989648891f192db862fefb67adc308df9b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } 199 \\qquad \\textbf{(B) } 200 \\qquad \\textbf{(C) } 398 \\qquad \\textbf{(D) } 399 \\qquad \\textbf{(E) } 400$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"446\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Note\">Note</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Note\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>This problem's solution logic is basically the same as <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=1991_AIME_Problems/Problem_4\">1991 AIME Q4</a>.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/8/5e89a998d86b7b9249187c37b13439759d5c8d34.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x)=\\sin(20\\pi x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"138\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/1/ca1048be08083846b4b20eee8fe48818ed623d86.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$g(x)=\\log_{20}(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"122\" height=\"20\" />. Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/1/311cabda3a9b09f0dde217303ca9d1cd9201dcf6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$g$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> passes through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/4/3c44a88b92f544ab76fcffd858f6e33972505e13.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(\\frac{1}{20},-1\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"81\" height=\"45\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/a/25a306093bbd9e0699c65dba033c9483cd379dc4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/5/025bced1994ba78c8fe3f60b8d7b4137b057cc4d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(20,1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"48\" height=\"18\" />; these are the extrema and midpoint of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" />. We want to find the number of intersections of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/1/311cabda3a9b09f0dde217303ca9d1cd9201dcf6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$g$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>Let an occurrence of sine passing under the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />-axis a <i>down-dip</i>, and similarly define an <i>up-dip</i>. We find that the period of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/0/4c00e9e2066800d5a55332a7fb456073bbce0a08.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{10}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"20\" height=\"37\" />, so between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/a/f0ad08889f005392f8a6e69f4439a39132af80b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\frac{1}{20}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"55\" height=\"37\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/5/4c52e02a35db152495396584bfeae1d0599cd753.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> the number of periods is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/c/aeca5e4a6bd9390dd1d4108eb103fff1c18424a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9.5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"25\" height=\"14\" />. The first period indeed counts, so effectively we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> down-dips in this interval. Each down-dip contributes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to the total, so we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/3/f8366cd6196dd8a9da6d38a3e9eafb109e99d53e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> total intersections.\n</p><p>From <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/5/4c52e02a35db152495396584bfeae1d0599cd753.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/f/1cf1342f460285dfd226094ac3973558576c9982.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=20$\" width=\"52\" height=\"12\" />, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/c/11c1903eedb7c6edd5aaa5fdbb609443458e6b2a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$190$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> periods, each of which also contributes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to the total due to the up-dips. Therefore, this case contributes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/3/c8362488734407d49f674714d31eeddef9fde314.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$380$\" width=\"26\" height=\"12\" /> points to the total.\n</p><p>But <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/a/25a306093bbd9e0699c65dba033c9483cd379dc4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" /> is counted in both cases, so the total is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/1/8d187151e8d03e6102e724f479b2eb575dc42587.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20+380-1=\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 399}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"199\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~ <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/User:Eevee9406\">eevee9406</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28AoPS_Precalculus.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(AoPS_Precalculus)\">Solution 2 (AoPS Precalculus)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (AoPS Precalculus)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Note that the number of solutions to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/0/c306f0b9328d962e79d0d443c1481c26ffefda96.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin(20\\pi x) = \\log_{20}(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"164\" height=\"20\" /> is the same as asking the number of intersection points of the graphs <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/6/fa65dade47ba3fc1198cfca415136e9fb9b3c03a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin(20\\pi x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"78\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/6/496b15748690da1ad4327b90cdb9c8628fadf5ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\log_{20}(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"64\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>We begin by understanding the range of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/6/fa65dade47ba3fc1198cfca415136e9fb9b3c03a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin(20\\pi x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"78\" height=\"20\" />. The range is just <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/a/c7aee59f867a29f18d65b609c8c6d8e93ffc62c7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-1 \\le \\sin(20\\pi x) \\le 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"159\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>For the graph <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/6/496b15748690da1ad4327b90cdb9c8628fadf5ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\log_{20}(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"64\" height=\"20\" /> to intersect <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/6/fa65dade47ba3fc1198cfca415136e9fb9b3c03a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin(20\\pi x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"78\" height=\"20\" />, we need <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/6/496b15748690da1ad4327b90cdb9c8628fadf5ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\log_{20}(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"64\" height=\"20\" /> to also be in the range <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/0/7b050d904e991ad639dc3020575be61b08228b4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-1 \\le \\log_{20}(x) \\le 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"145\" height=\"20\" />, which is simplified into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/2/fc2917aa376de8eb3fc0cd1ce3b1f87c3f87129b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{20} \\le x \\le 20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"101\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>The period of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/6/fa65dade47ba3fc1198cfca415136e9fb9b3c03a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin(20\\pi x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"78\" height=\"20\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/7/297e51124c4dcd4c994b0b51401d178953c68504.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2\\pi}{20\\pi} = \\frac{1}{10}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"81\" height=\"38\" />. Thus in every 1 unit, there are 10 periods.\n</p><p>Also, the x intersections of both graphs are the same. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/2/d22a0bc3c33ea30e7895a2712c4f0b3cb2a9a10b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin(20\\pi x) = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"111\" height=\"20\" /> indicates <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/c/d0c9ba281bd3a2b3cc14a6bbd2815f66ba466750.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = \\frac{1}{2} + k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"82\" height=\"38\" />. Also, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/1/391748ab997dbe5962fd98796ce1d3133c5510df.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\log_{20}(x) = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"97\" height=\"20\" /> if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/3/34310f2f36ed6d724838edb08788ee62acb33386.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"42\" height=\"12\" />, so we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/84574de7797bc7b4e1fc52075f4d265ba60a8bce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"14\" /> and we must consider two cases, either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/a/cfadc413775332669d83d1f51c260575bdd8ab3d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{20} \\le x \\le 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"92\" height=\"38\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/0/3f06f464eae85f35dd4c6314c89ed9b84a3b9cae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 &lt; x \\le 20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"88\" height=\"16\" />.\n</p><p>Case 1: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/a/cfadc413775332669d83d1f51c260575bdd8ab3d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{20} \\le x \\le 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"92\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>For this case, we first must understand one thing. Does <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/6/496b15748690da1ad4327b90cdb9c8628fadf5ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\log_{20}(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"64\" height=\"20\" /> intersect the first time <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/6/fa65dade47ba3fc1198cfca415136e9fb9b3c03a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin(20\\pi x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"78\" height=\"20\" /> dips below the x-axis for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/f/dff5a847457c4beaa2c6809fc9d7b26ebcaa398e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\ge 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"15\" /> 0 times, 1 time, or 2 times?\n</p><p>Well, notice that if we plug in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/3/053db0372730a6ac32da32dd53d49365c14a7bb4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = \\frac{1}{20}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"59\" height=\"38\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/2/d22a0bc3c33ea30e7895a2712c4f0b3cb2a9a10b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin(20\\pi x) = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"111\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/e/0fe1a28a93a70fef809afc32e3b6f32e151b3142.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\log_{20}(x) = -1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"111\" height=\"20\" />, so because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/6/496b15748690da1ad4327b90cdb9c8628fadf5ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\log_{20}(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"64\" height=\"20\" /> is increasing, it will intersect the graph of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/6/fa65dade47ba3fc1198cfca415136e9fb9b3c03a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin(20\\pi x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"78\" height=\"20\" /> twice.\n</p><p>Taking this into consideration, in each period, half the period is positive whereas the other half is negative. Then, because there are 10 periods between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/a/cfadc413775332669d83d1f51c260575bdd8ab3d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{20} \\le x \\le 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"92\" height=\"38\" />, there will be 10 negative periods.\n</p><p>Then, the graph <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/6/496b15748690da1ad4327b90cdb9c8628fadf5ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\log_{20}(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"64\" height=\"20\" /> intersects these 10 periods twice, giving us a total of 20 intersections in this case.\n</p><p>Case 2: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/0/3f06f464eae85f35dd4c6314c89ed9b84a3b9cae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 &lt; x \\le 20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"88\" height=\"16\" />.\n</p><p>For this case, we see that there is a total of 10 periods per unit, and there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/c/d7c6242ae03c9da5041d7d73910a3b42e42ba308.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20 - 1 = 19$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"91\" height=\"13\" /> units. Then, because once again there are 10 positive intervals, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/6/496b15748690da1ad4327b90cdb9c8628fadf5ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\log_{20}(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"64\" height=\"20\" /> will intersect each interval twice, giving us a total of 20 intersections per one unit. Multiplying by 19, we get a total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/b/f7b884868d02543527efbe635057683e54729ac4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$19 \\times 20 = 380$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"112\" height=\"15\" /> intersections. However, we have excluded 1, and the first period lasting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/b/91b079fbba5570865b6a21712e40b9ea626c3ada.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 \\le x \\le \\frac{11}{10}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"92\" height=\"38\" />, we see that two intersections occur, one at the point (1,0) and another at some random point. We already considered the point (1,0) last case, so we subtract 1 from the total to obtain <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/c/a1cd8e1c927036bbb81cb3483dd6be082b832947.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$380 - 1 = 379$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"112\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>Thus the total number of solutions to the equation is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/d/bddd4261006ae1898ffe8dfa0c0b75d1d1639d7e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20 + 379 =$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"89\" height=\"15\" /><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/d/b4db0db8e72478396ed1fdb68ee94ab2b87a0455.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 399}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"76\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28quick_but_risky.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(quick_but_risky)\">Solution 3 (quick but risky)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (quick but risky)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Each period of the sin function is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/0/4c00e9e2066800d5a55332a7fb456073bbce0a08.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{10}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"20\" height=\"37\" />, so 200 periods, 2 intersections each (just draw it out) - 400. But there is 1 overlap in two periods at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/a/25a306093bbd9e0699c65dba033c9483cd379dc4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" />, so subtract 1 -&gt; 399 (can check end behavior if time)\n~ScoutViolet\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Unit-period_parametrization.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Unit-period_parametrization)\">Solution 4 (Unit-period parametrization)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Unit-period parametrization)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/b/38b63dc117bb3c422948752c29096ce2d8cd758f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-1\\le \\sin 20\\pi x=\\log_{20} x\\le 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"223\" height=\"19\" />, forcing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/9/a29c64d7777415d8ff1e47cccad4f605bd9625ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{20}\\le x\\le 20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"101\" height=\"38\" />. For simplicity, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/2/da2681f0816b01a47bc2cd86ad7211bc63bc1c21.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\pi t=20\\pi x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"92\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/b/30b94613f54b8e676db05c0f5a0df2cda0017457.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t=10x\\in\\left[ \\frac{1}{2},200 \\right]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"151\" height=\"45\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/b/72b366149097d27b2d8175c6e342f331c17a9938.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\pi t$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> is the unit-period parametrization, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/3/8f37626d49b4082c9102bf930bca51277ff2eb43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$200$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> full periods on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/7/017762d9849093df033dc1548e49e2d702003a24.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[0,200]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"56\" height=\"20\" />, and we check the closed intervals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/1/4311d48a2074314efdacd69a797cb9e8070a1656.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left[ \\frac{1}{2},1 \\right],[1,2],\\dots,[199,200]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"211\" height=\"45\" />. By sketching the graph, we see that when sine and log share the same sign, the sine will weave through the log twice for the non-degenerate case. The endpoints are also non-degenerate after verifying <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/d/f4db552e6c68c85c090acca1f512235aa90d19b4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\log\\left( \\frac{1}{20} \\right)=-1,\\log_{20}20=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"224\" height=\"45\" /> whereas <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/f/95fd4ca0a1b02300b763aba51a1355b7f8fc0ee6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin 20\\pi \\left( \\frac{1}{20} \\right)=\\sin 20\\pi(200)=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"261\" height=\"45\" />.\n</p><p>Because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/8/df8e4bd916e8e70f7d59ba0c76bf02cdb557091a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\log_{20}(x=1)=0=\\log_{20}(t=10)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"256\" height=\"20\" /> lands perfectly on an integer, by the intermediate value theorem, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/8/698c1c9cde3469a346091405ffbbcedf96609562.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\log$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"23\" height=\"16\" /> will only have at most two signs among <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/7/5f7f55c5f871b0106ab72305ee570ce482a5f71a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-,0,+$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"55\" height=\"17\" /> in each full period closed interval. We count <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/3/fe3d8684d31d1b2b5c7211e4615460033fb01224.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$200\\cdot 2=400$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"103\" height=\"14\" /> intersections. However, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/4/21460a1064b496932ab255e861498acf761f645f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t=10$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"48\" height=\"13\" /> is shared among <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/a/6eaff825e54f1121bfbe8b9d4595c2f1699c488e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[9,10]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"47\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/b/9cb994d8744ee718265b4e94473e2dc32f841f10.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[10,11]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"56\" height=\"20\" /> so we must subtract the overlap to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/b/87b0cfad5d519d7ee589ada8036d3641339b99d4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$400-1=\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 399}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"159\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~imosilver\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_5_.28Risky.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5_(Risky)\">Solution 5 (Risky)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5 (Risky)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We know that the period of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/6/fa65dade47ba3fc1198cfca415136e9fb9b3c03a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin(20\\pi x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"78\" height=\"20\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/4/a1474cb76d1f31e06d4d911a707abacba69f082f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2\\pi}{20\\pi}=\\frac{1}{10}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"81\" height=\"38\" />, and that the amplitude of the graph is 1. Now, we  need to find the values of x for which the graph of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/5/9c5909b3bc7ee5ad521df0a45ce3af39642b64be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=\\log_{20} x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"87\" height=\"19\" /> intersects with the upper and lower extrema of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/6/fa65dade47ba3fc1198cfca415136e9fb9b3c03a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin(20\\pi x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"78\" height=\"20\" />, or with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/3/6c3b4834614e5e7e189cb7d621cfb4fb0bd36dd7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/b/acbab939b716843df7cc6fdb3e9a0b91b2fea5db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"59\" height=\"17\" />. At <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/b/acbab939b716843df7cc6fdb3e9a0b91b2fea5db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"59\" height=\"17\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/f/0cfce4b679c309aee64b1a7297ed63d96b9a65f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=20^{-1}=\\frac{1}{20}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"119\" height=\"38\" />, and at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/3/6c3b4834614e5e7e189cb7d621cfb4fb0bd36dd7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/d/1fd9e55aaa88afd4476c1b93d93ab08840b83952.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=20^1=20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"105\" height=\"17\" />. We know that for each period of the sin graph, there will be 2 intersections, and there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/e/a6e2601f80a9a7c23e325917e7053b09db316a91.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{20}{\\frac{1}{10}}=200$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"76\" height=\"45\" /> periods of the sin graph from 0 to 20. Because there are 3 answer choices close to 400, we can test with smaller bounds using a line, and count the points. The line from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/f/0dfab2d0c5e5ae2b354bb2672ef3a590b5f64af1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(\\frac{1}{20},-1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"69\" height=\"38\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/d/f4ddbc046000a18a6f70bcef8f0eaeeecd7e329a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(\\frac{1}{2},1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"46\" height=\"38\" /> intersected the graph <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/d/24d0b352fcf3381333da7c4c7163fa8fe9d7afd9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=\\sin(20\\pi x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"112\" height=\"20\" /> 9 times, and there were 5 periods between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, thus we can say the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/d/b4db0db8e72478396ed1fdb68ee94ab2b87a0455.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 399}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"76\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~Voidling\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1\">Video Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://youtu.be/q5_zsLoWuJ4?si=HSbtdYE5RELdQwJS\">2025 AMC 12 B #24</a>\n</p><p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://mathproblemsolvingskills.wordpress.com/\">MathProblemSolvingSkills.com</a>\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_2_by_OmegaLearn.org\">Video Solution 2 by OmegaLearn.org</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 2 by OmegaLearn.org\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/BzeEeUbjKDQ\">https://youtu.be/BzeEeUbjKDQ</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_3_by_MadMath\">Video Solution 3 by MadMath</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 3 by MadMath\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/YTBmIVrce6M\">https://youtu.be/YTBmIVrce6M</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span